      "message" : "Hi! Awesome setup — you’ve got a solid Proxmox homelab with real-world services (Nextcloud, Jellyfin, sports analytics, IdP, Win11). Building a **mini SOC (Security Operations Center)** for **practice + protection** is a *fantastic* idea. We’ll do this **methodically, safely, and with discovery built-in**.\n\n---\n\n## GOAL: Mini SOC for Proxmox Homelab\n> **Practice offensive/defensive security**  \n> **Harden your VMs**  \n> **Detect anomalies**  \n> **Respond to incidents**\n\n---\n\n## HIGH-LEVEL SOC ARCHITECTURE (Homelab Edition)\n\n| Component | Tool (Free/Open-Source) | Purpose |\n|--------|--------------------------|-------|\n| **SIEM** | Wazuh | Central log collection, alerting, FIM |\n| **IDS/IPS** | Suricata (or Wazuh HIDS) | Network + host intrusion detection |\n| **Endpoint Agent** | Wazuh Agent | On each VM |\n| **Network Sensor** | Wazuh + Zeek (optional) | Traffic analysis |\n| **Dashboard** | Wazuh Dashboard (Kibana-based) | Visualize alerts |\n| **Honeypot (optional)** | Cowrie | Lure attackers |\n| **Playbook/Response** | TheHive (optional) | Incident tracking |\n\nWe’ll start **lightweight** with **Wazuh** — it’s perfect for homelabs.\n\n---\n\n## PHASE 1: DISCOVERY & INVENTORY (Let’s Map Your Environment)\n\nWe’ll SSH into each VM and run discovery scripts.\n\n### Step 1: Create a Discovery Script (Run on Each Linux VM)\n\nSave as `/tmp/discover.sh` on **apps-ubuntu**, **IdP**, **sports-metrics**:\n\n```bash\n#!/bin/bash\necho \"=== SYSTEM INFO ===\"\nhostnamectl\necho -e \"\\n=== OS & KERNEL ===\"\ncat /etc/os-release | grep -E \"NAME|VERSION\"\nuname -r\n\necho -e \"\\n=== INSTALLED PACKAGES (Top 20 by size) ===\"\ndpkg-query -Wf '${Installed-Size}\\t${Package}\\t${Version}\\n' | sort -nr | head -20\n\necho -e \"\\n=== RUNNING SERVICES ===\"\nsystemctl list-units --type=service --state=running | grep \".service\"\n\necho -e \"\\n=== LISTENING PORTS ===\"\nss -tulnp | grep LISTEN\n\necho -e \"\\n=== SQL/MYSQL/POSTGRES? ===\"\nif command -v mysql &> /dev/null; then echo \"MySQL/MariaDB: $(mysql --version)\"; fi\nif command -v psql &> /dev/null; then echo \"PostgreSQL: $(psql --version)\"; fi\nif [ -f /etc/mysql/my.cnf ] || [ -f /etc/postgresql/*/main/postgresql.conf ]; then\n  echo \"DB Configs found!\"\nfi\n\necho -e \"\\n=== WEB SERVERS ===\"\nif command -v nginx &> /dev/null; then echo \"NGINX: $(nginx -v 2>&1)\"; fi\nif command -v apache2 &> /dev/null; then echo \"Apache: $(apache2ctl -v)\"; fi\n\necho -e \"\\n=== DOCKER? ===\"\nif command -v docker &> /dev/null; then\n  echo \"Docker: $(docker --version)\"\n  docker ps --format \"table {{.Names}}\\t{{.Image}}\\t{{.Status}}\\t{{.Ports}}\"\nfi\n\necho -e \"\\n=== CRON JOBS (root) ===\"\ncrontab -l 2>/dev/null || echo \"No root cron\"\n\necho -e \"\\n=== USERS WITH SHELL ===\"\ngetent passwd | awk -F: '$7 ~ /bash|sh/ {print $1}' | sort\n```\n\nMake executable and run:\n\n```bash\nchmod +x /tmp/discover.sh\nsudo /tmp/discover.sh > /tmp/discovery_report.txt\n```\n\n> **Send me the output** (or parts) — I’ll help interpret.\n\n---\n\n### For **Win11 VM** (RDP or Console)\n\nUse PowerShell:\n\n```powershell\nGet-ComputerInfo | Select WindowsProductName, OsVersion\nGet-Service | Where Status -eq Running | Select Name, DisplayName -First 20\nGet-NetTCPConnection -State Listen\nGet-Package | Sort InstalledSize -Desc | Select -First 20\nGet-Item \"C:\\ProgramData\\Microsoft\\Windows\\Start Menu\\Programs\\*.lnk\" -Recurse -ErrorAction SilentlyContinue\n```\n\n---\n\n## PHASE 2: UPDATE & PATCH EVERYTHING\n\n### Linux VMs:\n```bash\nsudo apt update && sudo apt full-upgrade -y\nsudo apt autoremove -y\nsudo reboot  # if kernel updated\n```\n\n### Win11:\n- Windows Update → Check for updates\n- Enable **Automatic Updates**\n\n---\n\n## PHASE 3: DEPLOY WAZUH (Your SOC Brain)\n\nWe’ll install **Wazuh All-in-One** on a **new lightweight VM** (recommended).\n\n### Create New VM: `soc-wazuh`\n- **OS**: Ubuntu 24.04 LTS (minimal)\n- **CPU**: 2 cores\n- **RAM**: 4 GB\n- **Disk**: 40 GB\n- **Network**: Bridged (same LAN as others)\n\n---\n\n### Install Wazuh All-in-One (on `soc-wazuh`)\n\n```bash\ncurl -sO https://packages.wazuh.com/4.x/wazuh-install.sh\nsudo bash ./wazuh-install.sh -a\n```\n\n> This installs:\n> - Wazuh Manager\n> - Filebeat\n> - OpenSearch\n> - Wazuh Dashboard\n\nAfter install:\n- Access: `https://<soc-ip>`\n- Default: `admin` / `admin` → change immediately!\n\n---\n\n## PHASE 4: INSTALL WAZUH AGENTS ON EACH VM\n\n### On **Linux VMs** (apps-ubuntu, IdP, sports-metrics):\n\n```bash\nWAZUH_MANAGER=\"192.168.x.x\"  # IP of soc-wazuh\ncurl -so wazuh-agent.deb https://packages.wazuh.com/4.x/apt/pool/main/w/wazuh-agent/wazuh-agent_4.8.2-1_amd64.deb\nsudo WAZUH_MANAGER=$WAZUH_MANAGER dpkg -i ./wazuh-agent.deb\nsudo systemctl start wazuh-agent\n```\n\n### On **Win11**:\n1. Download agent: https://packages.wazuh.com/4.x/windows/wazuh-agent-4.8.2-1.msi\n2. Install → Enter Wazuh Manager IP\n3. Start service\n\n> All agents will appear in Wazuh Dashboard → **Agents**\n\n---\n\n## PHASE 5: HARDENING CHECKS (Wazuh Will Help)\n\nWazuh includes **SCA (Security Configuration Assessment)** with CIS benchmarks.\n\n### Enable SCA Policies (Wazuh Dashboard)\n- Go to **Modules → Security Configuration Assessment**\n- Enable for Ubuntu, Windows\n\nYou’ll get:\n- Weak passwords?\n- Open ports?\n- Outdated packages?\n- SSH root login?\n\n---\n\n## PHASE 6: DETECTION RULES & ALERTS\n\nWazuh comes with 1000+ rules. Key ones for you:\n\n| Threat | Wazuh Detects |\n|-------|---------------|\n| Brute force SSH | Yes |\n| Web shell upload (Nextcloud) | Yes |\n| Malware (Win11) | Via VirusTotal integration |\n| File changes in `/var/www` | FIM |\n| Suspicious cron jobs | Yes |\n\n### Add Custom Rule (Example: Nextcloud data dir monitoring)\n\nEdit on `soc-wazuh`:\n```bash\nsudo nano /var/ossec/etc/rules/local_rules.xml\n```\n\n```xml\n<group name=\"nextcloud,\">\n  <rule id=\"100001\" level=\"12\">\n    <if_sid>550</if_sid>  <!-- File modified -->\n    <field name=\"file\">^/var/www/nextcloud/data/</field>\n    <description>CRITICAL: Nextcloud user data modified!</description>\n  </rule>\n</group>\n```\n\nRestart: `sudo systemctl restart wazuh-manager`\n\n---\n\n## PHASE 7: NETWORK VISIBILITY (Optional but Cool)\n\n### Add Zeek (via Wazuh or separate VM)\n\nOn `soc-wazuh` or new VM:\n\n```bash\nsudo apt install zeek -y\nsudo zeekctl install && sudo zeekctl start\n```\n\nSPAN/TAP your Proxmox bridge → send to Zeek → logs to Wazuh.\n\nDetect:\n- C2 beacons\n- Data exfil\n- Lateral movement\n\n---\n\n## PRACTICE: SIMULATE ATTACKS\n\nNow the **fun part** — red team yourself!\n\n### Safe Attacks to Try:\n1. **SSH Brute Force** → `hydra -l user -P /usr/share/wordlists/rockyou.txt ssh://<ip>`\n2. **Upload PHP shell** to Nextcloud (as user)\n3. **Run Mimikatz** on Win11 (in isolated VM)\n4. **Add malicious cron** as root\n\n→ Watch Wazuh **light up** with alerts!\n\n---\n\n## FINAL HOMELAB SOC DASHBOARD\n\n```\n[ Proxmox Host ]\n     │\n ├───[ apps-ubuntu ] ← Wazuh Agent\n ├───[ IdP ]         ← Wazuh Agent\n ├───[ sports-metrics ] ← Wazuh Agent\n ├───[ win11 ]       ← Wazuh Agent\n └───[ soc-wazuh ]   ← Wazuh Server + Dashboard\n```\n\n---\n\n## NEXT STEPS (Your Homework)\n\n1. **Run discovery script on all Linux VMs** → paste output here\n2. **Create `soc-wazuh` VM**\n3. **Install Wazuh all-in-one**\n4. **Deploy agents**\n5. **Take screenshot of Wazuh Dashboard with agents**\n\n---\n\n## BONUS: Future Upgrades\n| Tool | Purpose |\n|------|-------|\n| **TheHive + Cortex** | Incident response |\n| **Velociraptor** | DFIR |\n| **Caldera** | Automated adversary emulation |\n| **Security Onion** | Full SO alternative |\n\n---\n\nLet me know:\n- IPs of your VMs\n- Discovery output\n- Any services you *know* are running (Nextcloud on 80? Jellyfin on 8096?)\n\nAnd I’ll give you **custom Wazuh rules + hardening checklist**.\n\nYou're building a **real SOC** — this is pro-level stuff. Let’s keep going! 🛡️"
