      "message" : "### Weather Forecast for São Paulo (Interlagos) on November 9, 2025 – F1 Grand Prix Day\n\nThe 2025 F1 São Paulo Grand Prix is scheduled for Sunday, November 9, at 17:00 BRT (local time, UTC-3), with the race running approximately 71 laps over about 2 hours. Interlagos is notorious for variable weather, and forecasts indicate a mostly dry but cooler day with lingering morning moisture. Conditions should improve toward race time, potentially favoring slick tires, though a brief shower can't be ruled out. Here's a breakdown based on current meteorological data:\n\n| Time Period | Conditions | Temperature | Precipitation Chance | Wind | Humidity |\n|-------------|------------|-------------|-----------------------|------|----------|\n| **Morning (until ~12:00 BRT)** | Mostly cloudy with a slight to moderate risk of light rain or drizzle. Possible patchy fog in low-lying areas around the circuit. | 13–16°C (55–61°F) | 20–40% (light showers possible, <2mm total) | Light south-easterly, 5–10 km/h (3–6 mph) | 80–90% |\n| **Afternoon (12:00–17:00 BRT, pre-race)** | Clearing skies with increasing sunshine. Dry and stable, ideal for setup and warm-up laps. | 17–19°C (63–66°F) | <10% | Light south-easterly, 10–15 km/h (6–9 mph) | 70–80% |\n| **Race Time (17:00–19:00 BRT)** | Mostly clear to partly cloudy. Minimal disruption expected, but monitor for isolated light showers. Cooler temps may affect tire warm-up. | 18–19°C (64–66°F) | 10–20% (very slight chance of one quick shower) | Light south-easterly, 10–15 km/h (6–9 mph), gusts up to 20 km/h | 65–75% |\n| **Evening (post-race)** | Clear and cooling quickly. Dry overnight. | 15–17°C (59–63°F) | <5% | Calm to light, 5–10 km/h (3–6 mph) | 75–85% |\n\n#### Key Insights for the Grand Prix:\n- **Overall Outlook**: Cooler than the weekend's earlier days (which saw warmer, more humid starts), with low rain risk during the race window. This contrasts with Saturday's unsettled conditions (higher rain and wind for Sprint/Qualifying). Expect a track temperature around 25–30°C at lights out, aiding grip but requiring careful management of cooler air.\n- **Potential Impacts**: Any early rain could leave a damp track, leading to strategic tire choices (intermediates early, then slicks). Winds are mild, unlikely to cause major issues like last year's gusts.\n- **Sources & Updates**: Forecasts from the Brazilian National Institute of Meteorology (INMET), Formula1.com, and AccuWeather models as of November 8. Weather can shift rapidly at Interlagos—check live radar apps like Windy or the official F1 app for real-time updates during the event.\n\nIf you're attending, pack layers for the chill and a lightweight rain jacket just in case. Expect vibrant crowds and that classic Brazilian energy regardless!"
