      "message" : "SuperGrok, as my Newfoundland Labor Attorney, please help. \n\nRequest for consultation – resignation, demand letter, and potential claims (Canada Fluorspar NL)\n\nHi SuperGrok,\n\nI’m looking to book a consultation regarding my recent resignation from Canada Fluorspar (NL) Inc. (“CFI”), a demand letter I received from them, and my potential exposure and/or claims. I’m based in Newfoundland and Labrador and was employed in St. Lawrence, NL.\n\nI’d like advice on:\n\nThe enforceability of my notice obligations and my risk given their “breach of contract / damages” language.\n\nWhether CFI’s conduct could amount to constructive dismissal and/or a breach of their duty to accommodate my mental health and family situation.\n\nWhether their post-resignation actions cross into harassment or bad-faith conduct.\n\nBackground\n\nRole and tenure\n\nPosition: IT Coordinator (effectively sole internal IT for the company).\n\nDuties: Servers, networking, M365, security, telematics, access control, policy work, and numerous operational projects (I can provide details).\n\nI signed an employment letter and hire-on package in July 2024 that includes a clause saying I “may terminate” my employment by providing one month’s written notice and assisting with handover, and a standard “return all company property on termination” clause.\n\nWorkload, raise, and accommodation\n\nMay 2025: I requested a raise reflecting the scope and responsibility of my role. I never received a meaningful follow-up.\n\nSeptember 2025: When my fiancée returned to work, I requested schedule/work-from-home accommodation due to childcare and mental health. Instead of flexibility, I was effectively locked into 8:00–4:30 on-site with no WFH option.\n\nThrough 2025: My workload was extremely high (one-person IT function), with constant interruptions and pressure to “drop everything” for urgent issues. I have been seeing a therapist for work-related mental health issues for most of this year.\n\nWarnings before resignation\n\nOn a Friday roughly one week before I resigned, I explicitly told the HR Manager (Colleen Cashin) that I was on the verge of quitting.\n\nOn the following Thursday, I emailed Colleen and the CFO (Jerome Klopper) stating that changes needed to be made or I would need to start a handover process / consider leaving.\n\nThere was no meaningful change in workload, support, or accommodation.\n\nResignation and employer’s response\n\nLater that week, I submitted my resignation by email. Given my mental health and the circumstances, sick leave was my next option if I did not resign.\n\nApproximately 22.5 hours after my resignation email, HR emailed me acknowledging/accepting my resignation, asking (informally) why I left, requesting the office door code, and requesting login information for accounts I managed (e.g., LinkedIn, Indeed).\n\nAround three days after my resignation, I received a second email from HR with a formal letter attached, copied to CFI’s General Counsel (Niel Jacobs).\n\nThe demand letter (Dec 1, 2025)\nThe letter, prepared with involvement from General Counsel, does the following:\n\nAsserts that I am in “breach” of my employment contract for not providing one month’s written notice.\n\nRelies on the clause that says I “may terminate” by providing one month’s written notice and assisting with handover.\n\nCites the “return all company property” clause and extends it to “access credentials, passwords, authentication details, and admin rights.”\n\nClaims they made multiple unsuccessful attempts to obtain passwords, door code, etc.\n\nDemands that I immediately provide:\n\nThe office door code.\n\nAll usernames/passwords/authentication methods for any systems or platforms I created, accessed, or administered on CFI’s behalf (including LinkedIn, Indeed, “any IT systems,” website tools, etc.).\n\nAny other access information required to secure or operate CFI systems.\n\nStates that my “abrupt departure” and alleged failure to provide these credentials has caused, or may cause, “cost exposure,” and that CFI reserves the right to recover IT/consulting costs or other damages and to pursue all remedies if I do not comply.\n\nAccess / accounts\n\nAfter my resignation, CFI disabled my access to their systems and accounts (mailbox, admin consoles, etc.).\n\nI do not currently have access to CFI systems and I am not holding any CFI passwords or access credentials.\n\nLinkedIn super-admin access for the company page was transferred to our external IT provider prior to my departure; other corporate accounts were handled under my CFI email, which they control.\n\nInternal Teams logs (very important context)\nI have exported/saved Microsoft Teams conversations between:\n\nHR (Colleen Cashin)\n\nGeneral Counsel (Niel Jacobs)\n\nCFO (Jerome Klopper)\n\nRelevant excerpts include:\n\nHR telling General Counsel that I have “medical issues” and had reached a “breaking point” because of my workload and people constantly coming to my office expecting me to “drop everything” while I was doing critical work.\n\nGeneral Counsel replying immediately with “Oh fucking calm down” in response to HR’s description of my health and breaking point.\n\nGeneral Counsel and HR explicitly discussing “refer to these 2 clauses when drafting his letter of demand” and “that way we make it clear that he is in breach,” which suggests they were structuring the letter to frame me as being in breach rather than neutrally assessing the situation.\n\nI can provide screenshots/PDFs of these logs with timestamps and names.\n\nMy current position\n\nI have drafted a very short reply to HR that:\n\nAcknowledges receipt of the demand letter.\n\nProvides the office door code.\n\nStates that since my resignation I have not accessed CFI systems and that I am not in possession of any CFI passwords or access credentials (including for LinkedIn, Indeed, etc.).\n\nI have not yet sent this reply and have not engaged with them beyond reading the emails.\n\nMy mental health has been significantly affected by this job, and my therapist can likely provide documentation covering most of this year.\n\nWhat I am asking you for\n\nIn a consultation, I’m hoping you can:\n\nAssess how much real legal risk I face regarding:\n\nThe one-month notice clause (“may terminate by providing one month’s written notice”) and their “breach / damages” assertions.\n\nTheir ability to recover IT/consulting costs or other damages from me in practice.\n\nAdvise on whether my situation may support:\n\nConstructive dismissal (given workload, ignored raise, refused/limited accommodation, and mental health impact).\n\nA human-rights-type claim related to failure to accommodate a mental health condition and family/childcare needs.\n\nAny additional claims (e.g., aggravated / mental distress damages, or harassment / bad faith) given the Teams logs and demand letter.\n\nAdvise on my communication strategy going forward:\n\nWhether to send the short reply I’ve drafted, or modify it.\n\nWhether you recommend sending a preservation / litigation hold letter to CFI regarding emails, Teams logs, etc.\n\nAt what point I should stop replying at all and direct them to communicate through counsel.\n\nDocuments I can provide in advance\n\nI can send you, ahead of any call:\n\nEmployment letter (July 2024).\n\nHire-on / policy package (confidentiality, etc.).\n\nMy resignation email.\n\nHR’s “acknowledge your resignation” email (approx. 22.5 hours later).\n\nThe formal “letter of demand” from HR/General Counsel (Dec 1, 2025).\n\nScreenshots/PDFs of the Microsoft Teams logs described above.\n\nAny emails regarding my raise request, accommodation request, and statements that I was on the verge of quitting.\n\nA simple timeline summarizing the above.\n\nIf you are able to take this on, please let me know your consultation availability and whether you’d like me to send the documents and a timeline in advance.\n\nThank you for your time and consideration."
