      "message" : "In 2025, the emulation community primarily relies on a few trusted, community-vetted sources for ROMs rather than scattered individual sites, which often come with ads, malware risks, or unreliable files.\n\nThe most popular and recommended approach is the **r/Roms megathread** (hosted on GitHub or GitLab mirrors like r-roms.github.io). It's continuously updated by the community and points to direct download servers like **Myrient** (a high-speed, ad-free archive) and **Internet Archive** collections. These host verified No-Intro and Redump sets—the gold standard for accurate, complete ROM dumps across consoles like NES, SNES, N64, GameCube, Wii, PlayStation, Dreamcast, and more.\n\n### Why this is the go-to:\n- Clean, no pop-ups or viruses (unlike many older sites).\n- Organized by console, with options for full sets or individual games.\n- Supports formats like RVZ for Dolphin emulator (smaller files).\n- Frequently referenced in emulation forums and Reddit threads as the safest current option.\n\nOther commonly mentioned safe alternatives include:\n- **Vimm's Lair**: Long-standing, ad-light site with manual verification (hashes provided for integrity checks). Great for older consoles.\n- **Archive.org**: Legal for abandonware/public domain titles, plus large emulation collections.\n\nMany older \"ROM sites\" (e.g., ROM Hustler, CoolROM, Gamulator) are still around but often criticized for heavy ads, potential malware, or incomplete/outdated files. The community consensus has shifted toward the megathread archives for reliability.\n\n**Important note**: Downloading ROMs for games you don't own is generally illegal in most countries due to copyright. The safest and fully legal way is to dump your own physical cartridges/discs using tools like a modded console or dumper hardware. Always use antivirus and verify file hashes when downloading."
