      "message" : "Yeah, that's a solid, level-headed take. Durability matters way more in the long run—especially for a position like shortstop where you're grinding through 150+ games a year with constant lateral movement, quick bursts, and wear on the legs. Bo had a strong bounce-back 2025 (.311 AVG, 18 HR, 94 RBI, led MLB in hits and doubles), but the injuries piled up again: that major left PCL sprain on Sept 6 (home plate collision vs. Yankees) sidelined him for the final ~20 regular-season games, the entire ALDS and ALCS, and he only returned limited in the World Series (mostly DH/second base shifts, compromised defensively). Earlier in the year, right knee flare-up in late June/early July kept him out a few days too. He still played 139 games in the regular season (solid count, not total washout), but the pattern of lower-body stuff (knees, calves, quads recurring over years) makes him a risk for missing chunks when it counts most—like missing key playoff series.\n\nThe Jays proved they could win deep without him fully healthy (that WS run to Game 7 was gritty), leaning on Vlad's monster year, the rotation holding up, and depth guys stepping up. Now with Vlad locked in long-term as the core, plus additions like Cease for the rotation and other depth pieces this offseason, they're built for consistent contention without banking on someone who's shown he can get dinged up repeatedly.\n\nIf Bo stays mostly healthy in NY (new turf at Citi Field might help a bit, plus shifting to third could reduce some strain), great for him—he gets the big market, fresh start. But if the issues follow (and history suggests they might), the Mets could be the ones paying a premium for limited availability. Jays dodged a potential long-term albatross contract and kept flexibility. Smart baseball move in the end.\n\nYou cool with the current infield setup (Vlad at 1B, maybe Clement/others filling in at SS/3B), or you hoping they chase one more big bat this offseason before spring?"
