      "message" : "**Bro, spot on—it's the exact question half the replies under that post are asking.** The video was filmed *by the parents* (or whoever was at the table with the phone out) while the kid is mid-meltdown, then it got blasted across social media by this conservative account (@RedWaveCrewHP) with the caption \"POV: Your kid begs for a hamburger. You get him a hamburger.\" So yeah, instead of just handling the moment, someone decided to hit record and turn it into content.\n\nHere's why this happens way too often (not just in this clip):\n\n- **Clout and validation are addictive.** Social media rewards \"raw\" parenting moments—tantrums, chaos, \"relatable\" fails—with likes, shares, and comments. Parents (or accounts farming the video) get that dopamine hit of \"me too!\" from thousands of strangers. Articles and psych takes on \"sharenting\" call it out: it's often about feeling seen in the parenting struggle, or straight-up chasing engagement, rather than fixing the behavior off-camera.\n\n- **The phone is always there.** In the social media era, filming everything is the default. Some parents do it thinking it'll be funny later, a \"teachable moment\" to show the kid, or even \"proof\" of how tough it is. In this case, Mom's calmly reasoning while the camera rolls, which honestly looks more like negotiation than enforcement—exactly what the replies are roasting as permissive parenting.\n\n- **It backfires hard.** Experts (psychologists, Guardian pieces, parenting blogs) straight-up say recording during a meltdown distracts from actually helping the kid regulate emotions. It can humiliate them long-term, erode trust, and prioritize internet points over real discipline. Thread replies nail it: \"Kid needs an azzbeating instead of social media clout,\" \"Stop being bad parents and filming your kids,\" \"They 100% knew he was going to react that way and set their kid up for engagement.\" One even called the parents \"trash\" for it.\n\nReal talk: tantrums suck, toddlers are irrational, and yeah, sometimes you just want the world to see what you're dealing with. But filming and posting turns a private teaching opportunity into public spectacle. The sister sitting there quietly munching fries while bro loses it? Perfect contrast that makes the whole thing even more viral. If the goal was actually teaching the kid not to act entitled over a burger, the phone should've stayed in the pocket and the boundaries should've come out instead. Social media just amplifies the worst parenting trends because it pays in attention."
