      "message" : "**No video or clip was attached**, so I can't definitively say whether *that specific motion* was a balk. Drop the link, describe what the pitcher did (or which game/player), and I’ll break it down rule-by-rule.\n\n### Quick MLB Balk Rules Refresher (Official Baseball Rules 6.02(a) / 8.05)\nA **balk** is called when the pitcher (with runner(s) on base) makes an **illegal or deceptive motion** that could fool the runner(s). The ball becomes dead, every runner advances one base, and the pitch (if thrown) is nullified.\n\nHere are the most common triggers umpires look for:\n\n1. **No complete stop in the Set Position**  \n   Pitcher must come to a **full, discernible stop** with hands together before starting the delivery. Any twitch, rock, or continued movement after “coming set” = balk.\n\n2. **Motion naturally associated with the pitch but no delivery**  \n   Starts the wind-up or delivery motion toward home but stops or does something else.\n\n3. **Feint to 1st or 3rd without completing the throw**  \n   Starts a throwing motion to 1st or 3rd but doesn’t actually throw the ball.\n\n4. **Doesn’t step directly toward the base before throwing to it**  \n   Especially common on pickoffs. The pitcher’s free foot must land **toward the base** he’s throwing to (not just spinning or stepping toward home).\n\n5. **Throws or feints to an unoccupied base** (with exceptions)  \n   Can’t just fling it to an empty base to deceive runners.\n\n6. **Other classics**:\n   - Quick pitch (delivers before batter is reasonably set)\n   - Drops the ball while touching the rubber\n   - Makes any movement that deceives the runner while not actually pitching or throwing to a base\n\n### Gray Areas (Where Debates Happen)\n- **Slide step / quick delivery** — Some umps are stricter than others on whether there was a true stop.\n- **High leg kick or spin moves** — Direction of the leg/shoulder matters a lot.\n- **Shoulder turn or head movement** while in set position.\n- Pickoff attempts where the first baseman isn’t actually covering the bag (ump can call it if it looks like a fake to deceive the runner).\n\nUmpires have some discretion on the “deceptive” part, which is why you see so many “Was that a balk?” arguments.\n\n**Send me the clip** (or describe the exact motion — e.g., “lefty in set, lifted leg toward home then spun to first without stepping”) and I’ll give you a clear yes/no + which rule it violated (or why it was legal). ⚾\n\nWhat play are we looking at?"
